WEB_HELP = {'auto_haptics_enable': "Whether audio-derived haptics are off, mixed with the game's native output, or fully replace it.", 'auto_haptics_gain': 'Overall strength of the audio-derived haptics.', 'auto_haptics_smooth': 'How gradually the derived haptics rise and fall - higher is smoother, lower is punchier.', 'auto_haptics_gate': "The level below which derived haptics stay silent, so quiet audio doesn't buzz.", 'mix_native_level': "How much of a game's own native haptics reaches the actuators in Mix mode.", 'mix_native_filter': 'Whether the native feed is low-passed before mixing, or passed through untouched.', 'ah_dsp_source': 'Which audio channels the derived haptics are generated from.', 'auto_haptics_lowpass_hz': 'The frequency above which audio is ignored when looking for bass to drive haptics.', 'ah_xover_hz': 'Splits the haptic band so each grip is driven by a different part of the spectrum.', 'ah_low_gain': 'Strength of the low half of the split - impacts and deep rumble.', 'ah_high_gain': 'Strength of the high half of the split - the music and voice range.', 'auto_haptics_slope': 'How sharply the low-pass cuts off the unwanted higher frequencies.', 'auto_mute_replace': 'Mutes the controller speaker automatically in Replace mode.', 'auto_mute_mix': 'Mutes the controller speaker automatically in Mix mode.', 'tilt_steer_y': "Adds leaning the controller forward and back to the left stick's Y, on its own switch so it does not affect games where only steering matters. Meant for bikes and anything with a weight shift — in MotorStorm it is how you lean a bike in the air.", 'tilt_steer_y_amount': 'The most lean may add, as a percentage of full stick. Separate from the steering amount, because how much lean a game wants is not how much steering it wants.', 'tilt_steer_y_invert': 'Flip which way a lean pushes.', 'tilt_steer': 'Roll the controller like a small steering wheel and it ADDS to the left stick, rather than replacing it — coarse steering stays on the stick and tilt supplies trim on top. The angle comes from gravity, so it never drifts and always returns to centre when you level the pad. Calibrate it with the live test on the Macros tab: roll the pad and watch the L stick figure move, so you can see what the game will actually receive.', 'tilt_steer_range': 'How far you have to roll the pad for the full effect. Smaller means a small tilt does more.', 'tilt_steer_amount': 'The most tilt may add, as a percentage of full stick. Racing games apply their own steering dead zone, so anything below about 60 does nothing you can feel. Around 70 gives usable trim on top of the stick; 100 makes tilt able to steer on its own.', 'tilt_steer_deadzone': 'Degrees around level that do nothing, so the way you naturally hold the pad does not steer for you.', 'tilt_steer_invert': 'Flip which way a roll steers.', 'touch_mouse': 'Use the touchpad as a trackpad: the pointer follows how far your finger MOVES, like a laptop touchpad. Clicks are not part of this — bind the touchpad-click halves as macros with a mouse output, which lets you choose which half is left and which is right. Turning this on adds the mouse interface, so the device re-enumerates once.', 'touch_mouse_sens': 'Pointer speed. 100 is roughly 1:1 with the pad; raise it to cross the screen in one swipe.', 'touch_mouse_min': 'Ignore finger movement smaller than this many pad counts, so a resting finger does not make the pointer drift. 1 or 2 is usually enough.', 'touch_mouse_invert': 'Flip the pointer direction on either axis.', 'touch_mouse_trackball': 'Keep the pointer gliding after you lift your finger, so a flick can cross a large screen without repeated drags.', 'touch_mouse_friction': 'How quickly the glide slows down. Higher stops sooner.', 'player_led_mode': 'The five white LEDs under the touchpad. Normally they show a player number, which on a single-player PC tells you nothing. “Passthrough” leaves them to the game and controller; “Off” turns them off; “Battery gauge” fills one LED per 20% from the left, pulses the next one up while charging, and blinks the first alone below 20%. Taking them over means the game can no longer show a player number. On a DualSense Edge the controller also drives these for its on-board profiles — whether a profile change overrides the gauge is not yet known.', 'player_led_bright': 'How bright the player LEDs are, independently of the lightbar. In Passthrough the game still chooses which LEDs are lit, but the brightness set here is kept even when a game sets its own. Bright leaves brightness to the game entirely.', 'trigger_reduce': "Asks the controller to weaken its adaptive-trigger motors, in ten steps. Because the reduction happens inside the controller, it applies to whatever is driving the triggers — a game's native effects included — without changing their shape, only their force. Useful for games whose native trigger resistance is heavier than you want. Off leaves the triggers at full strength.", 'lightbar_off': "Keeps the controller's lightbar dark, in every haptics mode. A battery notification still overrides it for the seconds it runs.", 'rumble_haptic_strength': "Strength of a game's rumble re-created on the actuators when it can't be handed to native rumble.", 'effect_leak_volume': "How loudly sharp one-off sounds leak to the speaker while it's muted.", 'effect_leak_sensitivity': 'How readily a sound counts as a sharp onset worth leaking.', 'effect_leak_decay': 'How quickly a leaked accent fades once the sound passes.', 'effect_leak_attack': 'How fast the leak opens when an onset is detected.', 'effect_leak_output_hp_hz': 'Trims low frequencies from the leaked sound to keep the accent crisp.', 'effect_leak_hp_hz': 'The low edge of the band the detector listens to for onsets.', 'effect_leak_lp_hz': 'The high edge of the leaked sound - the top of the leak window.', 'effect_leak_max_burst': 'Caps how long one leak lasts, turning a sustained sound into a short accent.', 'effect_leak_hold': "Briefly keeps the leak open after a sound so it doesn't flutter.", 'haptics_gain': "Boosts the strength of a game's own native haptics.", 'speaker_volume': "Volume of the controller's built-in speaker.", 'headset_volume': 'Volume of a headset plugged into the controller.', 'speaker_gain': 'Extra pre-amplification applied to the speaker signal.', 'sync_spk_headset_volume': 'Ties speaker and headset volume together so one control moves both.', 'lock_volume': "Prevents the controller's volume buttons from changing the level.", 'disable_mic': 'Turns the controller microphone off.', 'disable_speaker': "Turns the controller's built-in speaker off.", 'controller_mode': 'Which controller model the dongle presents itself as to the PC.', 'polling_rate_mode': 'How often the controller reports to the PC - higher is more responsive.', 'audio_buffer_length': 'Audio buffering size - lower is lower-latency, higher is steadier.', 'inactive_time': 'Idle minutes before the controller disconnects to save battery.', 'disable_inactive_disconnect': 'Keeps the controller connected even when left idle.', 'disable_pico_led': "Turns off the Pico board's on-board LED.", 'enable_wake': 'Lets a PS-button press wake the PC from sleep (set per profile).', 'haptics_aa': 'How much the native haptics are smoothed to remove digital grit.', 'r2t_mode': "Turns a game's rumble into trigger vibration, and on which trigger(s).", 'r2t_on_press': 'Only vibrate the trigger while it is actually being pressed.', 'r2t_strength': 'Strength of the rumble-driven trigger vibration.', 'r2t_frequency': 'Frequency of the trigger vibration - lower feels heavier.', 'synth_force': 'Forces trigger effects on even when a game or app sends its own.', 'at_mode': "When R2's resistance is active - off, always, or gated by another input.", 'at_strength': 'How hard the R2 resistance pushes back.', 'at_threshold': "How far the gating input must be pressed to arm R2's resistance.", 'at_start_pos': "The trigger position where R2's resistance begins.", 'at_shape': "The shape of R2's resistance - flat, ramping, a detent, or a breakable wall.", 'at_strength_b': 'The end or wall strength used by the ramp and detent shapes.', 'at_detent_pos': 'The trigger position of the detent or break point.', 'at_deadzone': 'Ignores the first part of the pull so the game only registers a press past this zone.', 'at_pushback': 'Strength of a recoil kick fired at the break point (0 = no kick).', 'at_kick_style': 'Whether the recoil kick is a buzz or a mechanical push-back.', 'at_pushback_freq': "Frequency of the kick's thump - lower feels heavier.", 'at_pushback_src': 'What the recoil kick is driven from (shared by both triggers).', 'ce_r2_enable': 'Enables a captured custom effect on R2 (assigned in the monitor below).', 'ce_r2_condition': 'Whether the custom effect plays while held, on press, or on release.', 'ce_r2_thresh': 'The engage point, or for while-held walls the zone where the wall re-arms.', 'ce_r2_rate': 'How fast a two-state vibration effect toggles between its states.', 'ce_r2_state_count': 'How many states make up the custom effect (set automatically).', 'ce_r2_yield': 'Who owns R2 when gated - the custom effect or the resistance sliders.', 'at_l2_mode': "When L2's resistance is active - off, always, or gated by another input.", 'at_l2_strength': 'How hard the L2 resistance pushes back.', 'at_l2_threshold': "How far the gating input must be pressed to arm L2's resistance.", 'at_l2_start_pos': "The trigger position where L2's resistance begins.", 'at_l2_shape': "The shape of L2's resistance - flat, ramping, a detent, or a breakable wall.", 'at_l2_strength_b': 'The end or wall strength used by the ramp and detent shapes.', 'at_l2_detent_pos': 'The trigger position of the detent or break point.', 'at_l2_deadzone': 'Ignores the first part of the pull so the game only registers a press past this zone.', 't2_mode': "Turns the pull into two stages: the trigger below the boundary, a button above it. 'Rescale' stretches the shorter travel back over the full range so you keep full throttle authority. 'Swap' releases the trigger when the button engages - needed for alt-fire, wrong for racing.", 't2_pos': 'Where the second stage begins, in raw trigger counts (0-255). Set an adaptive-trigger wall at the same point so you can feel the boundary.', 't2_button': 'The controller button the second stage presses. A real gamepad button, so games keep showing controller prompts.', 't2_l2_mode': "Turns the pull into two stages: the trigger below the boundary, a button above it. 'Rescale' stretches the shorter travel back over the full range so you keep full throttle authority. 'Swap' releases the trigger when the button engages - needed for alt-fire, wrong for racing.", 't2_l2_pos': 'Where the second stage begins, in raw trigger counts (0-255). Set an adaptive-trigger wall at the same point so you can feel the boundary.', 't2_l2_button': 'The controller button the second stage presses. A real gamepad button, so games keep showing controller prompts.', 'at_l2_pushback': 'Strength of a recoil kick fired at the break point (0 = no kick).', 'at_l2_kick_style': 'Whether the recoil kick is a buzz or a mechanical push-back.', 'at_l2_pushback_freq': "Frequency of the kick's thump - lower feels heavier.", 'ce_l2_enable': 'Enables a captured custom effect on L2 (assigned in the monitor below).', 'ce_l2_condition': 'Whether the custom effect plays while held, on press, or on release.', 'ce_l2_thresh': 'The engage point, or for while-held walls the zone where the wall re-arms.', 'ce_l2_rate': 'How fast a two-state vibration effect toggles between its states.', 'ce_l2_state_count': 'How many states make up the custom effect (set automatically).', 'ce_l2_yield': 'Who owns L2 when gated - the custom effect or the resistance sliders.', 'gyro_mode': 'When gyro aiming is active - off, always, or only while a chosen button is held.', 'gyro_sens': 'How much aim movement the gyro produces.', 'gyro_axis': 'Whether turning or tilting the controller drives horizontal aim. “Player space” uses the accelerometer to work out which way is down and combines both, so the aim behaves the same whether the pad is held flat or tilted up — no need to pick an axis and then hold the controller a fixed way.', 'gyro_sens_mode': 'Manual is the original slider: a number with no real-world meaning, retuned per game. Natural makes it a RATIO - at 1.0x, rotating the controller 10 degrees turns the view 10 degrees - so one setting is correct in every game that shares the same counts per 360. Natural needs the counts per 360 value below to be right, and applies to gyro-to-MOUSE only: gyro-to-stick tells the game how FAST to turn, not how far, so a rotation ratio has nothing to attach to there.', 'gyro_natural_x10': 'The ratio, x10. 10 means 1:1 - the view turns exactly as far as you rotate the pad. Most people play between 25 (2.5x) and 120 (12x), higher the less they rely on the stick. To check it is truly 1:1 on your hardware, use the degrees readout in the Device tab: rotate through a known angle and compare.', 'gyro_natural_y_x10': "Vertical ratio, x10. 0 follows the horizontal one. A game's vertical aiming range is much smaller than its horizontal one, so a lower value here is common.", 'flick_angle': 'How far a full sideways flick turns the view. Flick Stick is absolute by design — push right and you face right, 90° — which is what makes a smaller check impossible. This scales it, so the direction still means something while the amount is yours. 90 is the original behaviour; 45 turns half as far for the same stick push. It scales rotating a held stick by the same factor, so a flick and the turn back stay consistent.', 'flick_counts_360': "How far a flick turns you, in this game. It is the same number a real mouse needs to spin a full 360 degrees: (cm per 360) x (DPI / 2.54). If you know your cm/360 and DPI, just calculate it - no trial needed. Otherwise leave 6500 and correct once: flick fully back (a 180), see how far you actually turned, then multiply this value by 180 / the degrees you got. Two tries is usually enough. Turn mouse acceleration OFF and raw input ON, or no single value can be correct. Changing the game's own sensitivity means recalibrating.", 'gyro_sens_y': 'Vertical gyro sensitivity on its own. Leave at 0 and both axes use the Sensitivity setting above, exactly as before. A lower value here is the usual choice: the vertical aiming range in a game is far smaller than the horizontal one, so the gain that feels right for turning is often too fast for looking up and down.', 'gyro_output': 'Right stick works in any game with no setup. Mouse sends pointer movement instead, which is what a gyro naturally produces - finer at low speed and it never pegs on a fast turn - but many games ignore mouse input while a gamepad is present, so it usually needs the pad hidden. Changing this re-enumerates the controller.', 'gyro_invert': "Flips the direction gyro aiming moves - X, Y, or both. Only affects gyro, when it's on.", 'rstick_invert': 'Inverts the physical right stick itself - X, Y, or both - in any game, separate from gyro.', 'bt_flush_timeout': 'Caps how long Bluetooth batches reports - lower cuts latency but can drop packets.', 'bt_qos_latency_us': 'Requests a Bluetooth latency target - aggressive is snappier but less reliable.'}
